## TL;DR  

*The shield layers that come with the OpenMapTiles-based “OSM Bright” (and the very similar “OSM Liberty”) style look for the following **sprite-image IDs**:*

| Sprite sheet (default) | Image IDs that are queried |
|------------------------|----------------------------|
| **US Interstate**      | `us-interstate_1` , `us-interstate_2` , `us-interstate_3` |
| **US Highway** (U.S. highways, e.g. US-20) | `us-highway_1` , `us-highway_2` , `us-highway_3` |
| **US State** (state routes, e.g. CA-99) | `us-state_1` , `us-state_2` , `us-state_3` , `us-state_4` , `us-state_5` , `us-state_6` |
| **All other networks** (e.g. European E-roads, provincial roads, etc.) | `road_1` , `road_2` , `road_3` , `road_4` , `road_5` , `road_6` |

*If any of those IDs are missing from the sprite that your style loads, the shield’s badge disappears and you only see the numeric `text-field` (the “bare floating number”).*  

The fix is **not** to edit the style layers – you only need to make sure the sprite sheet you point to actually contains those IDs (or add them yourself at runtime).

Below is a step-by-step guide that shows:

1. **Where to find a ready-made sprite that already contains the needed IDs** (the official OSM Bright / OSM Liberty sprite).  
2. **How to self-host that sprite** (or any custom sprite you build).  
3. **How to add the missing badge images on the fly with `addImage` / `addSprite`** if you cannot change the `sprite` URL.  
4. **How to use the new “multiple-sprite-sheet” array form** to keep the original basemap sprite and just add a second sheet that supplies the missing badge images.

---

## 1. What the style actually asks for

### OSM Bright (the style shipped with the OpenMapTiles demo)

The three shield layers are defined (simplified) as:

```json
{
  "id": "highway-shield-us-interstate",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "openmaptiles",
  "source-layer": "transportation_name",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}",
    "text-field": "{ref}"
  }
},
{
  "id": "highway-shield-us-highway",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "openmaptiles",
  "source-layer": "transportation_name",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}",
    "text-field": "{ref}"
  }
},
{
  "id": "highway-shield-us-state",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "openmaptiles",
  "source-layer": "transportation_name",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}",
    "text-field": "{ref}"
  }
},
{
  "id": "highway-shield-generic",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "openmaptiles",
  "source-layer": "transportation_name",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "road_{ref_length}",
    "text-field": "{ref}"
  }
}
```

* `network` is a string such as `us-interstate`, `us-highway`, `us-state`, or any other network name.  
* `ref_length` is an integer (1-6) that tells the style how many digits the road number has.  
* The **concatenated string** becomes the sprite-image ID that the layer tries to draw.

Hence the exact IDs you must have in the sprite are exactly the table above.

### OSM Liberty (the “liberty” style that ships with the same tileset)

It uses a single generic badge:

```json
"icon-image": "default_{ref_length}"
```

So the required IDs are:

```
default_1 … default_6
```

If you are using the Liberty style, replace the “road_*” list with the “default_*” list.

---

## 2. Use a sprite that already contains those IDs

The **official OSM Bright sprite** (and the matching Liberty sprite) are published together with the style:

| Style | Sprite base URL (no extension) |
|-------|--------------------------------|
| OSM Bright | `https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite` |
| OSM Liberty| `https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-liberty-gl-style/sprite` |

Both URLs resolve to:

* `…/sprite.json` – the index that lists every image ID (including all the `us-*` and `road_*` entries).  
* `…/sprite.png` – the PNG atlas that actually contains the badge graphics.

**If you point your style’s `sprite` property at one of those URLs, the shields appear automatically.**  

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite",
  ...
}
```

> **Important:** Do **not** use the raw-GitHub URL (`raw.githubusercontent.com/.../sprite.json`) in production – it is rate-limited and has a 5-minute cache. The `github.io` domain is a proper static-file host with CDN caching and CORS headers.

### Self-hosting the official sprite

If you want to avoid any third-party host, just copy the two files (`sprite.json` and `sprite.png`) plus the `@2x` variants (`sprite@2x.json`, `sprite@2x.png`) to your own static file server and point `sprite` at that location.

```text
https://my.cdn.example.com/osm-bright/sprite   ← base URL
```

Make sure:

* The URL **has no file extension** (MapLibre will add `.json`, `.png`, `@2x.json`, `@2x.png` automatically).  
* The server sends **CORS headers** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain).  
* The files are served with a **200** status and correct MIME types (`application/json` and `image/png`).

---

## 3. Adding the missing badge images without touching the style

If you cannot change the `sprite` URL (e.g. you are using a hosted style that you cannot edit) you have two options:

### 3.1. Load the missing images at runtime with `addImage`

```js
map.on('styleimagemissing', async (e) => {
  // e.id is the missing image name, e.sourceId is undefined for sprites
  const missingId = e.id;               // e.g. "us-interstate_2"
  const base = 'https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite';

  // Build the URL for the PNG atlas that contains the image.
  // The easiest way is to request the whole sprite PNG and then crop,
  // but MapLibre already knows how to extract a single image from the atlas.
  // The API `map.loadImage` can fetch a *single* image if you give it the
  // full sprite URL plus the image name as a query param (MapLibre 6+):
  //   `${base}.png?name=${missingId}`
  // However, most browsers don’t support that, so we fetch the whole PNG
  // and let MapLibre slice it for us via `addImage`.

  // Simpler: just fetch the PNG of the whole sheet and let MapLibre
  // locate the sub-image using the sprite JSON we also fetch.
  const jsonUrl = `${base}.json`;
  const pngUrl  = `${base}.png`;

  // Load the sprite JSON once (cache it)
  if (!window._osmBrightSprite) {
    const resp = await fetch(jsonUrl);
    window._osmBrightSprite = await resp.json();
  }
  const spriteIndex = window._osmBrightSprite;

  // If the missing ID exists in the index, we can extract it.
  if (spriteIndex[missingId]) {
    const { data } = await map.loadImage(pngUrl); // whole PNG
    // `addImage` can take a sub-image definition via the `options` argument:
    const { x, y, width, height, pixelRatio } = spriteIndex[missingId];
    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
    canvas.width = width;
    canvas.height = height;
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    ctx.drawImage(
      data,
      x, y, width, height,   // source rect
      0, 0, width, height    // destination rect
    );
    const img = new Image();
    img.src = canvas.toDataURL();
    img.onload = () => {
      if (!map.hasImage(missingId)) {
        map.addImage(missingId, img, { pixelRatio });
      }
    };
  } else {
    console.warn('Sprite does not contain', missingId);
  }
});
```

**What this does**

* The map fires `styleimagemissing` for each badge it cannot find.  
* The handler fetches the *official* OSM Bright sprite JSON/PNG (once) and extracts the missing sub-image.  
* `addImage` registers the image under the exact same ID (`us-interstate_2`, `road_4`, …).  
* The layer that originally failed now finds the image and re-renders automatically.

> **Tip:** If you have many missing images you can pre-load the whole sprite once and call `addImage` for every ID you need, instead of waiting for the `styleimagemissing` event for each one.

### 3.2. Add a second sprite sheet with the missing IDs (MapLibre 3.0+)

Since MapLibre 3.0 the `sprite` property can be an **array of sprite sheets**. You can keep the original basemap sprite (which may contain all the POI icons you like) and *add* a second sheet that only contains the shield badges.

```json
{
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/sprites/ofm_f384/ofm" },
    { "id": "shields", "url": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite" }
  ],
  ...
}
```

* All shield IDs are now addressed as `shields:us-interstate_2`, `shields:road_3`, etc.  
* **BUT** the existing style layers use the un-prefixed names (`us-interstate_2`). To keep them working you must make the *second* sheet the **default** one, or add a *prefix* to the layer definitions. The easiest way is to **swap the order** and give the shield sheet the `id: "default"`:

```json
{
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite" },
    { "id": "extra",   "url": "https://tiles.openfreemap.org/sprites/ofm_f384/ofm" }
  ]
}
```

Now the shield layers resolve against the default sheet (which contains the badge images) and the rest of the style still finds its own icons in the `extra` sheet via the `extra:` prefix (if any). If you don’t need the extra sheet you can drop it entirely.

**Adding the sheet from code (after the style has loaded):**

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // Add the shield sprite as a second sheet
  map.addSprite('shields', 'https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite');
  // If you want it to be the default (no prefix) you can replace the existing one:
  // map.setStyle({ ...style, sprite: [{id:'default',url:'...'}] });
});
```

> **Note:** `addSprite` is a **runtime** API that works only after the style’s `load` event. It does **not** replace the original `sprite` property in the style JSON; it merely loads the extra sheet into

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste HTML page** that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6** and shows how to

1. **Load 40 + SVG icons** that live on your own domain.  
2. **Register each SVG with `map.addImage()`** (runtime images).  
3. **Add a GeoJSON source** that contains ~5 000 venue points, each with a `category` property.  
4. **Draw the venues as a symbol layer** whose `icon-image` is taken from the `category` field.  
5. **Let the style’s collision detection hide overlapping icons** when you are zoomed out (the default behaviour).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 (you can also use a CDN version you prefer) -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Initialise the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    // a minimal style – you can replace this with any style you like
    version: 8,
    sources: {},
    layers: [],
    // IMPORTANT: we do **not** set a sprite here because we are using
    // only runtime images (addImage).  If you also want a basemap,
    // add a raster/vector source + layer or point the style at a sprite.
  },
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],   // San Francisco (change to your area)
  zoom: 12,
  hash: true
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  List of category → SVG URL
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const categories = {
  // key = value of the `category` property in your GeoJSON
  // value = absolute URL to the SVG file (must be CORS-enabled)
  // Example (add all 40+ you need):
  "cafe":      "https://mydomain.com/icons/cafe.svg",
  "park":      "https://mydomain.com/icons/park.svg",
  "museum":    "https://mydomain.com/icons/museum.svg",
  "restaurant":"https://mydomain.com/icons/restaurant.svg",
  "library":   "https://mydomain.com/icons/library.svg",
  // … continue for every category you have …
};

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Helper: load a single SVG as an Image element
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function loadSvg(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    // The SVG must be served with CORS headers (Access-Control-Allow-Origin)
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(new Error(`Failed to load SVG ${url}`));
    img.src = url;
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Load *all* SVGs, register them with the map, then add data
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.once('load', async () => {
  try {
    // ---- 4a. Load every SVG in parallel ---------------------------------
    const loadPromises = Object.entries(categories).map(
      async ([id, url]) => {
        const img = await loadSvg(url);
        // `addImage` will reject if the id already exists – guard with hasImage()
        if (!map.hasImage(id)) {
          // No SDF (distance-field) – we keep the original colours.
          // If you want the icons to be recolourable with `icon-color`,
          // rasterise them as SDF (see the spec) and set `sdf: true`.
          map.addImage(id, img);
        }
      }
    );

    await Promise.all(loadPromises);
    console.log('✅ All SVG icons added to the style');

    // ---- 4b. Add the GeoJSON source ------------------------------------
    // Replace the URL with your own endpoint or inline GeoJSON.
    map.addSource('venues', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://mydomain.com/data/venues.geojson'   // 5 000 features
    });

    // ---- 4c. Add the symbol layer ---------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'venue-icons',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'venues',
      // The `icon-image` expression pulls the icon name from the feature's
      // `category` property.  It must match the keys we used above.
      layout: {
        // Use the property directly – MapLibre will look up the image by that name.
        'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],

        // OPTIONAL: give the icons a little padding so they don’t touch the edge
        // of the map when they’re near the viewport border.
        'icon-allow-overlap': false,   // default, kept for clarity
        // If you want the icons to stay visible even when they collide,
        // set this to true.  The requirement in the question is the opposite.
        // 'icon-allow-overlap': true,

        // A small size works well for many points; adjust to taste.
        'icon-size': 0.8,

        // Keep the icons upright (no rotation with the map)
        'icon-rotate': 0,

        // If you have a lot of points, you can improve performance a bit
        // by disabling the text halo (we have no text here) and setting a
        // modest `symbol-sort-key` so that higher-zoom features win.
        'symbol-sort-key': ['zoom']   // simple: larger zoom = higher priority
      },
      // No `paint` needed unless you want a halo or opacity.
    });

    console.log('✅ Venue layer added');
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('❌ Something went wrong while loading icons or data:', err);
  }
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  Optional: show a loading spinner while the map is busy
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('data', (e) => {
  if (e.sourceId === 'venues' && e.isSourceLoaded) {
    // hide spinner if you added one
  }
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does, step-by-step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Creates a `maplibregl.Map` instance. No sprite is defined because we only use runtime images. | Keeps the style minimal; you can replace the empty style with any vector/raster basemap you like. |
| **2️⃣ Category → SVG map** | A plain JavaScript object where the **key** matches the `category` property in your GeoJSON and the **value** is the absolute URL of the SVG. | `icon-image` will later read the `category` property and look up the image by that exact key. |
| **3️⃣ `loadSvg()`** | Returns a `Promise` that resolves to an `HTMLImageElement` once the SVG has loaded (CORS-enabled). | `map.addImage()` expects an `Image` (or `ImageBitmap`) – the helper abstracts the async loading. |
| **4a. Load & register all SVGs** | `Promise.all()` loads every SVG in parallel, then registers each with `map.addImage(id, img)`. | One PNG sprite would have required a build step; `addImage` lets you keep the SVGs separate and host them yourself. |
| **4b. Add the GeoJSON source** | Points are added via a `geojson` source (`venues`). | The source can be a remote URL or an inline object; MapLibre will fetch it once. |
| **4c. Symbol layer** | `icon-image` is set to `['get', 'category']`. The layer inherits the default collision-detection (`icon-allow-overlap: false`). | When you zoom out, overlapping icons are automatically hidden, satisfying “icons shouldn’t pile up”. |
| **5️⃣ Optional spinner** | Shows how you could react to the source-loaded event. | Improves UX for large datasets. |

### Tweaking the behaviour

| Need | Change |
|------|--------|
| **Make icons larger/smaller** | Adjust `'icon-size': 0.8` (0 = invisible, 1 = original SVG size). |
| **Force all icons to be drawn (no collision culling)** | Set `'icon-allow-overlap': true` (or `'icon-overlap': 'always'`). |
| **Prioritise certain categories** | Use a more sophisticated `symbol-sort-key` expression, e.g. `['case', ['==', ['get', 'category'], 'cafe'], 2, 1]`. |
| **Re-colour SVGs with `icon-color`** | Convert the SVGs to **SDF** (distance-field) format and register them with `{sdf: true}`; then you can set `paint: { 'icon-color': '#ff6600' }`. |
| **Add a halo around the icons** | Only works on SDF icons: `paint: { 'icon-halo-color': '#fff', 'icon-halo-width': 1 }`. |
| **Use a basemap style** | Replace the empty `style` object with a URL, e.g. `style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json'`. The runtime images will still be available because they are added **after** the style loads. |

---

#### Gotchas & Debugging

* **CORS** – The SVG files must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). Otherwise `map.addImage()` will throw a security error.
* **Missing image warning** – If you see `Image "<id>" could not be loaded` in the console, double-check that the `category` value exactly matches a key in `categories` and that the SVG URL is reachable.
* **Performance** – 5 000 points is fine for a symbol layer. If you ever go to > 50 000, consider clustering or using a vector tile source instead of raw GeoJSON.

That’s it! Drop the snippet into a file, replace the placeholder URLs with your own SVG and GeoJSON locations, and you’ll have a map where each venue shows its category icon and overlapping icons are automatically hidden when you zoom out. Happy mapping!

## TL;DR  

*The current heuristic (`text-anchor` missing **and** `symbol-placement === "point"`) is **not reliable** for detecting route-shield layers.*  
*Shield layers are identified by a **combination of layout properties that are unique to shields**, not by the absence of `text-anchor`.*

Below is a quick-read why the heuristic fails, followed by a **robust decision-tree** you can embed in your audit script, plus a **minimal code example** that works with both the old “string-sprite” style and the newer “multiple-sprite-sheet” style.

---

## 1. Why the current rule is unsound

| Property | What the rule checks | What you actually see in real-world styles |
|----------|----------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| `text-anchor` | “undefined → shield” | **Almost every symbol layer** (place-names, POIs, traffic signs, etc.) **does not set `text-anchor`** because the default (`"center"`) is used. The property is omitted in the JSON, so `layer.layout['text-anchor']` is `undefined` for *all* of them. |
| `symbol-placement` | Must be the literal string `"point"` | Shield layers **often use a *zoom-function* or an *expression* that switches between `"point"` and `"line"`** (e.g. OSM Bright’s interstate shield). In the style JSON this appears as an object with `stops` or as an expression array, **not** as the plain string `"point"`. Consequently the rule will **miss** those shields. |
| Result | Plain place-name layers (which also omit `text-anchor` and have `symbol-placement: "point"`) are flagged as shields, while many genuine shield layers are ignored. | ✅  |

In short: **absence of `text-anchor` is not a shield signal**, and **exact string equality on `symbol-placement` is too strict**.

---

## 2. What *does* make a shield layer unique?

A route-shield layer in the OpenMapTiles / OSM-Bright / OSM-Liberty families shares **four** hallmarks:

| Hallmark | Typical value / pattern | Why it matters |
|----------|------------------------|----------------|
| **`icon-image`** | An expression that resolves to a *sprite name* containing a **placeholder for `ref_length`** (e.g. `"{network}_{ref_length}"`, `"road_{ref_length}"`, `"default_{ref_length}"`). | The badge image is chosen based on the length of the road reference. |
| **`text-field`** | References the route number: `"{ref}"` (or an expression that resolves to `{ref}`) | The number that sits on top of the badge. |
| **`icon-text-fit`** (optional but common) | `"both"` or `"width"` (sometimes `"height"`). When present, the badge is *stretchable* and the text is forced to fit inside it. | Only shields (or other “badge-style” symbols) need this; ordinary POI icons never set it. |
| **`icon-image`** **and** **`text-field`** both **use data-driven placeholders** (`{…}`) – i.e. they are **expressions**, not static strings. | Place-name layers usually have a static `icon-image` (e.g. `"city"`), and their `text-field` is a property like `["get","name"]` (no `{ref}` placeholder). |
| **`icon-allow-overlap` / `icon-overlap`** | Often set to `true` or `"always"` for shields (so the badge isn’t hidden by other symbols). | Not required, but a useful secondary clue. |
| **`symbol-placement`** | Either `"point"` **or** a **zoom-function** that yields `"point"` at low zooms and `"line"` at higher zooms. | The presence of a *function* (object with `stops` or an expression array) is a strong indicator that the layer is a line-shield rather than a simple point label. |
| **Layer ID / source-layer name** | Frequently contains the word *shield* (`"highway-shield-us-interstate"`, `"road-shield"`). | Helpful for a quick “human-readable” filter, but not mandatory. |

If **any** of the first three hallmarks are present, you can safely treat the layer as a shield. The combination of **`icon-image` containing `{ref_length}`** **and** **`text-field` containing `{ref}`** is the *minimal* reliable signature.

---

## 3. A robust heuristic (decision-tree)

```text
Is layer.type === "symbol"? ──► NO → not a shield
│
├─► Does layer.layout have an "icon-image"?
│    └─► Is the value an expression (array) OR a string that contains "{ref_length}" ?
│          ──► NO → not a shield
│          │
│          └─► YES → continue
│
├─► Does layer.layout have a "text-field"?
│    └─► Is the value an expression (array) OR a string that contains "{ref}" ?
│          ──► NO → not a shield
│          │
│          └─► YES → continue
│
├─► (Optional) Does layer.layout["icon-text-fit"] exist and ≠ "none" ?
│    ──► YES → definitely a shield (stretchable badge)
│    ──► NO  → still a shield if the two previous checks passed
│
└─► (Optional) Does layer.layout["symbol-placement"] contain a function/object?
     ──► YES → likely a *line* shield (good to know for labeling logic)
```

**Key points**

* **Never rely on `text-anchor`** – it is omitted for the default value on *all* symbol layers.
* **Accept both plain strings and expressions** for `icon-image` / `text-field`.  
  *A plain string may already be the fully-resolved sprite name (e.g. `"us-interstate_2"`). In that case the string **contains** `_2` but not the placeholder; you can still treat it as a shield if the name matches the pattern `*_{1-6}` and the layer also has a `text-field` that pulls `{ref}`.*  
* **Check for the placeholder `{ref_length}`** (or the concrete pattern `_<digit>`). This is the only thing that distinguishes a shield badge from a generic POI icon.
* **If you need to differentiate point-vs-line shields**, inspect `symbol-placement`. A *function* (object with `stops` or an expression array) means the layer will render as a line-shield at higher zooms.

---

## 4. Concrete JavaScript implementation

Below is a **drop-in replacement** for the heuristic you currently use. It works with MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0 (multiple sprite sheets) and with older single-sprite styles.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route-shield layer.
 *
 * @param {Object} layer – a layer object from a style JSON (already parsed)
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 1️⃣ icon-image must exist and reference a shield badge
  const iconImg = layout['icon-image'];
  if (!iconImg) return false;

  // Helper: is the value an expression (array) or a plain string?
  const isExpr = Array.isArray(iconImg);
  const iconStr = isExpr ? (iconImg[1] || '') : iconImg; // crude extraction of the template part

  // Shield badge pattern: contains {ref_length} placeholder OR ends with _{1-6}
  const badgePattern = /\{ref_length\}|_[1-6]$/;
  if (!badgePattern.test(iconStr)) return false;

  // 2️⃣ text-field must exist and reference the route number
  const txt = layout['text-field'];
  if (!txt) return false;

  const txtIsExpr = Array.isArray(txt);
  const txtStr = txtIsExpr ? (txt[1] || '') : txt;
  if (!/\{ref\}/.test(txtStr)) return false; // missing {ref} placeholder

  // 3️⃣ (optional) icon-text-fit indicates a stretchable badge – strengthens confidence
  const textFit = layout['icon-text-fit'];
  if (textFit && textFit !== 'none') return true; // definitely a shield

  // 4️⃣ (optional) symbol-placement function → line-shield
  // (not required for detection, just for extra info)
  // const placement = layout['symbol-placement'];
  // if (placement && typeof placement === 'object' && !Array.isArray(placement)) {
  //   // it's a zoom function (object with stops) → line shield
  // }

  // If we got here, we have both a badge reference and a ref placeholder → shield
  return true;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Example usage on a whole style:
-------------------------------------------------------------- */
function auditStyleForShields(style) {
  const shieldLayers = style.layers.filter(isShieldLayer);
  console.log(`Found ${shieldLayers.length} shield layer(s):`);
  shieldLayers.forEach(l => console.log(' •', l.id));
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Test with a real style (you can paste the JSON or fetch it):
-------------------------------------------------------------- */
fetch('https://demotiles.maplibre.org/styles/osm-bright-gl-style/style.json')
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(style => auditStyleForShields(style));
```

### Why this works

| Check | What it catches | What it discards |
|-------|----------------|------------------|
| `icon-image` contains `{ref_length}` **or** ends with `_<digit>` | All OSM-Bright / OSM-Liberty shield badges (`us-interstate_2`, `road_3`, etc.) and any custom shield that follows the same naming convention. | POI icons (`cafe`, `park`, `city`) never contain that pattern. |
| `text-field` contains `{ref}` | Guarantees the layer is trying to render a route number on top of the badge. | Place-name layers use `{name}` or `["get","name"]`. |
| `icon-text-fit` ≠ `none` (optional) | Detects *stretchable* shields (`icon-text-fit: "both"`). | Still returns true for non-stretchable shields because the two required checks already passed. |
| `symbol-placement` function (optional) | Lets you separate *point* shields (e.g. highway-exit icons) from *line* shields (the classic road-shield that follows the line). | Not required for detection, but useful for downstream processing. |

---

## 5. Edge Cases & How to Handle Them

| Situation | Why it can slip through the net | Suggested tweak |
|-----------|--------------------------------|-----------------|
| **Custom shields that don’t use `{ref_length}`** (e.g. a single badge image named `"shield"` and `icon-text-fit` used to stretch it) | The badge name lacks the placeholder, so the first regex fails. | Add a fallback: if `icon-text-fit` is set **and** `text-field` contains `{ref}`, treat it as a shield even when the badge name lacks `{ref_length}`. |
| **Legacy styles that embed the badge name directly (`"us-interstate_2"`), not an expression** | The placeholder isn’t present, but the concrete name ends with `_<digit>`. The regex `_[1-6]$` already catches this. |
| **Shield layers that use a different placeholder name** (e.g. `{network}` + `{ref_len}`) | Unlikely in the official OSM-Bright/Liberty styles, but possible in a custom style. | Make the badge-pattern configurable: `new RegExp('\\{ref_length\\}|_\\d+$')` → allow the user to pass a custom pattern. |
| **Non-shield layers that happen to have a badge-like name** (e.g. a POI called `"road_2"`) | Very rare, but could happen if a data-driven icon name coincidentally matches the pattern. | Combine the check with `text-field` containing `{ref}` – POI layers usually reference `{name}` or a property that isn’t `ref`. |
| **Multiple-sprite-sheet styles** – the badge lives in a non-default sheet (`my-icons:us-interstate_2`) | The `icon-image` string will contain a colon (`:`). | The regex still works because it looks *after* the colon: `my-icons:us-interstate_2` matches `_[1-6]$`. No extra handling needed. |

---

## 6. Quick checklist for your audit script

| ✅ Should be true for a shield | ❌ Should be false for a plain

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** example that shows how to

1. **Create a draggable pin (Marker) at a known longitude/latitude**  
2. **Open a Popup when the user clicks the pin**  
3. **Keep the Popup anchored to the pin after it is dragged**  

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – draggable pin with popup</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link
    href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
    rel="stylesheet"
  />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // replace with your own style
    center: [-122.42, 37.77], // lon, lat – San Francisco for demo
    zoom: 12
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ When the style is ready, add the draggable marker
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // The coordinate where the pin should start
    const startLngLat = [-122.42, 37.77];

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2a️⃣ Create a Popup (but don’t add it yet)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
      offset: 25,               // distance from the marker tip
      closeButton: true,
      closeOnClick: false      // keep it open when the map is clicked
    }).setHTML(`
      <h4>My draggable pin</h4>
      <p>Click the map to move me, or drag the pin.</p>
    `);

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2b️⃣ Create the Marker – draggable = true
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
      draggable: true,          // <-- makes the pin draggable
      // You can also pass a custom HTML element here:
      // element: document.createElement('div')
    })
      .setLngLat(startLngLat)   // place it at the start coordinate
      .setPopup(popup)          // associate the popup with the marker
      .addTo(map);              // finally add it to the map

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Open the popup when the user clicks the pin
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // The Marker class already toggles the popup on click when you call
    // .setPopup().  If you want to force-open it immediately:
    // marker.togglePopup();

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Keep the popup anchored after a drag
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // When the marker is dragged we update the popup’s position.
    // (MapLibre does this automatically, but we can also listen for the
    //  dragend event if we need the new coordinates for something else.)
    marker.on('dragend', () => {
      const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
      console.log('Marker dropped at', lngLat);
      // If you need to store the new location, do it here.
      // The popup stays attached, no extra code required.
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ (Optional) – click-anywhere on the map to move the pin
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.on('click', (e) => {
      marker.setLngLat(e.lngLat);
      // If you want the popup to open automatically after a map click:
      marker.togglePopup();
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## Why this works (the concepts)

| Step | What it does | MapLibre API used |
|------|--------------|-------------------|
| **Create a `Popup`** | Holds the HTML you want to show. | `new maplibregl.Popup()` |
| **Create a `Marker` with `draggable:true`** | Makes the pin draggable with the mouse / touch. | `new maplibregl.Marker({draggable:true})` |
| **`setLngLat()`** | Positions the marker at a specific `[lon, lat]`. | `marker.setLngLat([lon, lat])` |
| **`setPopup(popup)`** | Binds the popup to the marker. Clicking the marker automatically opens / closes the popup. | `marker.setPopup(popup)` |
| **`addTo(map)`** | Adds the marker (and its popup) to the map canvas. | `marker.addTo(map)` |
| **`marker.on('dragend', …)`** | Fires after the user finishes dragging; you can read the new coordinates with `marker.getLngLat()`. | `marker.on('dragend', fn)` |
| **`map.on('click', …)`** (optional) | Demonstrates how to move the marker programmatically when the user clicks elsewhere on the map. | `map.on('click', fn)` |

### A few gotchas & best-practice notes

| Issue | What to watch for | Fix / tip |
|-------|-------------------|-----------|
| **Marker vs. Symbol layer** | A `Marker` lives **outside** the style (HTML overlay). Use it for a *few* interactive points. For thousands of points, use a **symbol layer** with a sprite or `addImage`. | Keep the number of markers low (< ~200) for good performance. |
| **Cross-origin images** | If you replace the default pin with a custom PNG/SVG hosted on another domain, the image must be CORS-enabled (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*`). | Host the image on the same origin or enable CORS on the remote server. |
| **SVG icons** | `Marker` can use any HTML element, so you can embed an `<svg>` directly. For a sprite-based symbol layer you must rasterise the SVG first (`addImage`). | Example: `marker.getElement().innerHTML = '<svg …></svg>';` |
| **Popup staying open after drag** | The popup is automatically re-anchored to the marker after a drag, but if you close it manually you may want to reopen it on `dragend`. | `marker.togglePopup();` inside the `dragend` handler if you need that behaviour. |
| **MapLibre version** | The example uses v4.x (the current stable at time of writing). The API is stable back to v2, but `map.loadImage` callback form was removed in v4. | If you are on an older version, adjust accordingly (e.g., use the callback form). |

---

## TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

If you already have a `map` instance and just need the core lines:

```js
// 1️⃣ Popup (HTML can be anything)
const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ offset: 25 })
  .setHTML('<strong>Draggable pin</strong>');

// 2️⃣ Draggable marker at a known coordinate
const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable: true })
  .setLngLat([lon, lat])   // ← replace with your lon/lat
  .setPopup(popup)         // ← click the pin → popup opens
  .addTo(map);

// 3️⃣ Optional: log new position after each drag
marker.on('dragend', () => console.log('new position:', marker.getLngLat()));
```

That’s all you need to have a **draggable pin** that **shows a popup on click** in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
